IMPORT prelude

-- Destitute Persons Act 1989 (Singapore) -- who is a "destitute person" and what begging is (s 2), being taken in and required to
-- reside in a welfare home (ss 3, 7(2)), the habitual-beggar offence (s 4), fingerprint and photograph records (s 6), periodic review
-- and discharge (ss 10, 11, 12(1), 20), the carer's 24-hour duty (s 12(2), (3)), deemed residence (ss 13(2), 14), and the offences of
-- leaving a home and disorderly behaviour, with arrest and return (ss 16 to 19).
--
-- Edition: 2020 Revised Edition, informal consolidation, as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/DPA1989.txt. The deposit says the
-- revised edition "incorporates all amendments up to and including 1 December 2021", is marked "Informal Consolidation -- version in
-- force from 1/7/2026", and annotates amendments by Act 5 of 2025 (wef 09/03/2025) and Act 14 of 2025 (wef 01/07/2026, the Social
-- Residential Homes Act 2025 changes).
--
-- WHY THIS ACT. Chosen for its everyday-life relevance: it governs what happens to a person found begging or sleeping rough in a public
-- place, and to the relative or friend who takes such a person home. It is a short Act and almost all of its operative provisions are
-- encoded. Not encoded: the Minister's powers to establish homes and make rules (ss 7(1), 9), the appointment and constitution of the
-- Review Committee beyond its review interval (s 10(1A), (2)), transfers between homes (s 15), the s 5 undertaking beyond its effect
-- on s 16, the work requirement in s 13(1), and everything delegated to the Social Residential Homes Act 2025 (licensing, the
-- "person-in-charge", "licensee" and "key appointment holder").
--
-- NAIVE ENCODING. Straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules skill. No pipeline, no coverage table,
-- no independent test pass, no human gate. See NOTES.md.

§ `Destitute Persons Act 1989 -- welfare homes`

GIVEN `a` IS A NUMBER, `b` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the smaller of` MEANS IF `a` AT MOST `b` THEN `a` ELSE `b`

§§ `2 -- destitute persons and begging`

-- s 2(1): "destitute person" means (a) "any person found begging in a public place in a way that causes or is likely to cause
-- annoyance to persons frequenting the place or otherwise creates a nuisance"; or (b) "any idle person found in a public place,
-- whether or not he or she is begging, who has no visible means of subsistence or place of residence or is unable to give a
-- satisfactory account of himself or herself".
-- "public place" includes "any place to which the public is admitted without payment or on payment".
--
-- READING CHOSEN for limb (b): "has no visible means of subsistence or place of residence" is read as having NEITHER (no means AND no
-- residence). The other reading (lacking EITHER) would make an idle person with a home but no visible income destitute. This is an
-- interpretive choice; the text does not settle it.
GIVEN `in a public place` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `begging` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `causing annoyance or a nuisance` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `idle` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `has visible means of subsistence` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `has a place of residence` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `can give a satisfactory account of themself` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is a destitute person` IF
        `in a public place`
    AND (    (`begging` AND `causing annoyance or a nuisance`)
         OR  (    `idle`
              AND (    ((NOT `has visible means of subsistence`) AND (NOT `has a place of residence`))
                   OR  (NOT `can give a satisfactory account of themself`))))

-- s 2(2): "a person is deemed to be begging if the person's conduct is calculated to induce the giving of alms, whether or not there
-- is any pretence of singing, playing, performing, offering anything for sale or otherwise". The pretence is irrelevant either way.
GIVEN `conduct calculated to induce alms` IS A BOOLEAN, `pretence of performing or selling` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is deemed to be begging` IF `conduct calculated to induce alms`

§§ `3 and 7(2) -- taken in, inquiry and residence warrant`

-- s 3(1): a public officer acting under the Director-General's direction, or any police officer, "may take in the officer's charge any
-- destitute person and deliver that person to the custody of the Director-General".
-- s 3(2): "Subject to section 7(2), if the Director-General has reasonable cause to believe that any person so delivered ... has no
-- visible means of subsistence", he or she may arrange temporary admission into a welfare home until an inquiry is held.
-- s 7(2): the Director-General "must not arrange for a person to be temporarily admitted into a welfare home or require a person to
-- reside in a welfare home unless the operation of the welfare home is authorised by a licence under the Social Residential Homes Act
-- 2025".
-- The s 3(2) test is narrower than the s 2 definition: only "no visible means of subsistence" counts. A person delivered for nuisance
-- begging who has visible means cannot be temporarily admitted under s 3(2).
GIVEN `delivered into the Director-General's custody` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `reasonable cause to believe no visible means of subsistence` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the home is licensed` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `temporary admission may be arranged` IF
    `delivered into the Director-General's custody` AND `reasonable cause to believe no visible means of subsistence` AND `the home is licensed`

-- s 3(3): "Every such inquiry must be completed within a period of 30 days from the date of that person's admission into a welfare home
-- or such further period, not exceeding 30 days, as the Minister may approve."
GIVEN `extension approved by the Minister in days` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the inquiry deadline in days` MEANS 30 PLUS `the smaller of` `extension approved by the Minister in days` 30

GIVEN `days since admission` IS A NUMBER, `extension approved by the Minister in days` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the inquiry is out of time` IF
    `days since admission` GREATER THAN `the inquiry deadline in days` `extension approved by the Minister in days`

-- s 3(4): "Subject to section 7(2), if after holding the inquiry the Director-General is satisfied that the person is a destitute person
-- within the meaning of this Act, the Director-General may by warrant under his or her hand require that person to reside in a welfare
-- home." No maximum period of residence is stated anywhere in the Act.
GIVEN `the inquiry was held` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `satisfied the person is destitute` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the home is licensed` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a residence warrant may issue` IF
    `the inquiry was held` AND `satisfied the person is destitute` AND `the home is licensed`

§§ `4 -- habitual begging`

-- s 4(1): "Any person being a habitual beggar found begging in a public place in a way that causes or is likely to cause annoyance ...
-- or otherwise creates a nuisance shall be guilty of an offence" -- fine not exceeding $3,000 or imprisonment not exceeding 2 years.
-- s 4(2): "habitual beggar" means a person who "on at least 2 previous occasions" was found nuisance-begging in a public place "and,
-- consequently, was required on those 2 occasions to reside in a welfare home".
-- Only occasions that ended in a requirement to reside count. A voluntary admission (s 5) is not a requirement to reside (inference
-- from the contrast between ss 3(4) and 5).
GIVEN `previous occasions that led to a requirement to reside` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is a habitual beggar` IF `previous occasions that led to a requirement to reside` AT LEAST 2

GIVEN `previous occasions that led to a requirement to reside` IS A NUMBER,
      `begging now` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `in a public place now` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `causing annoyance or a nuisance now` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `commits the begging offence` IF
        `is a habitual beggar` `previous occasions that led to a requirement to reside`
    AND `begging now` AND `in a public place now` AND `causing annoyance or a nuisance now`

§§ `6 -- fingerprints and photographs`

-- s 6(1): the Director-General may authorise the taking of finger impressions and photographs of a destitute person in a home.
-- s 6(2): "after the end of 2 years from the date of discharge ... the Director-General must, on that person's application, deliver to
-- that person the sheet upon which that person's finger impressions have been made, together with the negatives and copies of the
-- photographs", and "if no application is received after that period of 2 years and before the end of 3 years from the date of
-- discharge, those records must be destroyed".
-- s 6(3): "The Director-General may retain 3 copies of the photographs" for a register of destitute persons.
-- An application made before the 2 years end is not provided for; this encoding treats `applied in the window` as an application made
-- between the end of year 2 and the end of year 3.
DECLARE `Records outcome` IS ONE OF
    `held by the Director-General`
    `awaiting an application`
    `delivered to the person`
    `destroyed`

GIVEN `years since discharge` IS A NUMBER, `applied in the window` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Records outcome`
`what happens to the fingerprint records` MEANS
    IF `years since discharge` LESS THAN 2
    THEN `held by the Director-General`
    ELSE `after the two years` `years since discharge` `applied in the window`

GIVEN `years since discharge` IS A NUMBER, `applied in the window` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Records outcome`
`after the two years` MEANS
    IF `applied in the window`
    THEN `delivered to the person`
    ELSE IF `years since discharge` LESS THAN 3
         THEN `awaiting an application`
         ELSE `destroyed`

`photograph copies the Director-General may keep for the register` MEANS 3

§§ `10, 11, 12(1) and 20 -- review and discharge`

-- s 10(1): "The Minister may appoint a Review Committee which must review the case of every resident at intervals of not more than
-- 12 months to assess the resident's suitability for discharge" (inserted by Act 5 of 2025 wef 09/03/2025). The duty attaches to a
-- Committee once appointed; the appointment itself is discretionary ("may").
GIVEN `a Review Committee has been appointed` IS A BOOLEAN, `months since last review` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the review is overdue` IF
    `a Review Committee has been appointed` AND `months since last review` GREATER THAN 12

-- s 11: a resident "may be discharged by the Director-General either conditionally or unconditionally on the advice of the Review
-- Committee". s 20: "The Minister may at any time order any person admitted to a welfare home to be discharged."
GIVEN `the Review Committee advised discharge` IS A BOOLEAN, `the Minister ordered discharge` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a discharge route is open` IF `the Review Committee advised discharge` OR `the Minister ordered discharge`

-- s 12(1): where a relative or friend "who, in the opinion of the Director-General is willing and able to provide care and support"
-- desires the discharge, the Director-General "must" -- (a) upon the individual's application and (b) on the individual "giving
-- security to the satisfaction of the Director-General" -- order the discharge to that individual's care. No Review Committee advice
-- is needed on this route.
GIVEN `a relative or friend` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the Director-General thinks them willing and able` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `they applied` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `they gave satisfactory security` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Director-General must discharge to their care` IF
    `a relative or friend` AND `the Director-General thinks them willing and able` AND `they applied` AND `they gave satisfactory security`

§§ `12(2) and (3) -- the carer's 24-hour duty`

-- s 12(2): "If for any reason the care and support of the individual for the destitute person ceases, the individual must inform the
-- Director-General in writing within 24 hours of the cessation". s 12(3): failing to do so is an offence -- fine not exceeding $500 or
-- imprisonment not exceeding 2 months. No reasonable-excuse defence is written into the section.
GIVEN `the care has ceased` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `informed the Director-General in writing` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `hours after cessation when informed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the carer commits an offence` IF
        `the care has ceased`
    AND (    (NOT `informed the Director-General in writing`)
         OR  `hours after cessation when informed` GREATER THAN 24)

§§ `13(2) and 14 -- deemed residence`

-- s 13(2): a resident "who attends work outside the welfare home is deemed, while engaged in such employment, to be a resident of the
-- welfare home". s 14: a resident sent to hospital on the medical officer's direction "is deemed, while receiving such treatment, to be
-- a resident of the welfare home".
GIVEN `living in the home` IS A BOOLEAN, `working outside as a resident` IS A BOOLEAN, `in hospital on the medical officer's direction` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `counts as a resident` IF
    `living in the home` OR `working outside as a resident` OR `in hospital on the medical officer's direction`

§§ `16 -- leaving the home`

-- s 16: an offence for any person who (a) "escapes from the police while committed to the charge of the police under this Act"; (b)
-- "leaves a welfare home in which the person is required to reside in accordance with section 3 or to which the person has been
-- admitted on the person's own application under section 5, without the permission of the person-in-charge"; or (c) "having obtained
-- the permission of the person-in-charge ... to leave ... for a limited time or for a specified purpose, but fails to return".
-- Penalty: "imprisonment for a term not exceeding 6 months" -- no fine.
--
-- A voluntary resident (s 5) who walks out without permission commits the offence. Whether a person "temporarily admitted" under
-- s 3(2) pending inquiry is "required to reside in accordance with section 3" is not clear: s 3 uses "require ... to reside" only for
-- the s 3(4) warrant. This encoding reads (b) as NOT covering temporary admission (an interpretive choice), while (c) is not limited by
-- basis of admission.
DECLARE `Basis of residence` IS ONE OF
    `residence warrant under s 3(4)`
    `temporary admission pending inquiry`
    `own application under s 5`

GIVEN `basis` IS A `Basis of residence`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `paragraph (b) covers the basis` IF
    CONSIDER `basis`
    WHEN `residence warrant under s 3(4)` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `own application under s 5` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN `basis` IS A `Basis of residence`,
      `left with permission` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `returned as required` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `leaving is an offence` IF
        ((NOT `left with permission`) AND `paragraph (b) covers the basis` `basis`)
    OR  (`left with permission` AND (NOT `returned as required`))

§§ `17 -- disorderly behaviour`

-- s 17: an offence for a resident who (a) "takes part in any assault or attack on any officer of the welfare home"; (b) "aggravates or
-- repeatedly assaults any other person residing in the welfare home"; or (c) "wilfully destroys any property of the welfare home" --
-- fine not exceeding $3,000 or imprisonment not exceeding 2 years.
-- No offence in the Act attaches to refusing work under s 13(1) (rules under s 9 were not read).
DECLARE `Resident conduct` IS ONE OF
    `taking part in an attack on an officer`
    `aggravating another resident`
    `repeatedly assaulting another resident`
    `wilfully destroying home property`
    `accidentally breaking home property`
    `refusing to do assigned work`

GIVEN `conduct` IS A `Resident conduct`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is disorderly behaviour` IF
    CONSIDER `conduct`
    WHEN `accidentally breaking home property` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `refusing to do assigned work` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

§§ `Penalties, arrest and return -- ss 4, 12(3), 16 to 19`

DECLARE Offence IS ONE OF
    `habitual begging`
    `carer failing to report`
    `leaving the home`
    `disorderly behaviour`

GIVEN `offence` IS AN Offence
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum fine in dollars` MEANS
    CONSIDER `offence`
    WHEN `habitual begging` THEN 3000
    WHEN `carer failing to report` THEN 500
    WHEN `leaving the home` THEN 0
    WHEN `disorderly behaviour` THEN 3000

GIVEN `offence` IS AN Offence
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum imprisonment in months` MEANS
    CONSIDER `offence`
    WHEN `habitual begging` THEN 24
    WHEN `carer failing to report` THEN 2
    WHEN `leaving the home` THEN 6
    WHEN `disorderly behaviour` THEN 24

-- s 18: a person "reasonably suspected of having committed an offence under section 16 or 17 and who refuses or fails to accompany a
-- police officer to, or to appear before, a Magistrate's Court" may be arrested without a warrant.
-- s 19: a person who has completed a sentence under s 16 or 17 "must be returned to a welfare home".
GIVEN `offence` IS AN Offence
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is a section 16 or 17 offence` IF
    CONSIDER `offence`
    WHEN `leaving the home` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `disorderly behaviour` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN `offence` IS AN Offence, `refused or failed to accompany or appear` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `may be arrested without a warrant` IF
    `is a section 16 or 17 offence` `offence` AND `refused or failed to accompany or appear`

GIVEN `offence` IS AN Offence
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `must be returned to a welfare home after the sentence` IF `is a section 16 or 17 offence` `offence`
